        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.365,
            0.848,
            0.407
          ],
          "content": "？ Which scale helped you measure the length of the screws accurately? Why?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.429,
            0.774,
            0.468
          ],
          "content": "What is the meaning of $2 \\frac { 7 } { 1 0 }$ cm (the length of the first screw)?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.476,
            0.848,
            0.651
          ],
          "content": "As seen on the ruler, the unit length between two consecutive numbers is divided into 10 equal parts. To get the length $2   \\frac { 7 } { 1 0 }   \\mathrm { c m }$ , we go from 0 to 2 and then take seven parts of $\\frac{1}{10}.$ The length of the screw is 2 cm and $\\frac { 7 } { 1 0 }$ cm. Similarly, we can make sense of the length $3   \\frac { 2 } { 1 0 }   \\mathrm { c m }$ We read $2 \\frac { 7 } { 1 0 }$ cm as **two and seven-tenth centimeters**, and $3 \\frac { 2 } { 1 0 }$ cm as **three and two-tenth centimeters**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.667,
            0.847,
            0.709
          ],
          "content": "？ Can you explain why the unit was divided into smaller parts to measure the screws?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.727,
            0.848,
            0.789
          ],
          "content": "？ Measure the following objects using a scale and write their measurements in centimeters (as shown earlier for the lengths of the screws): pen, sharpener, and any other object of your choice."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.805,
            0.754,
            0.831
          ],
          "content": "？ Write the measurements of the objects shown in the picture:"
        },
        {
